<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanlin Academy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as College of Literature, Academy of the Forest of Pencils</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hanlin Academy was an academic and administrative institution founded in the 8th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> by Emperor Xuanzong of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. Its membership was confined to an elite group of scholars who did secretarial and literary work for the court, and one of its main duties was to settle the interpretation of the Chinese classics on which the imperial examinations for higher government posts were based. Court painters were attached to it, and its members included Li Bai, Su Shi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouyang-xiu">Ouyang Xiu</a>. From 1407 the Ming attached to it a Bureau of Translators for foreign languages. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> buildings and library burned in 1900, and it operated until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> of 1911.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy.jpg" alt="Hanlin Academy" width="330" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jin Kun, Sun Hu, Lu Zhan, Wu Yu, and Zhang Qi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanlin_Academy_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
